WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.437
Duplicates can quietly distort an analysis.

00:00:03.437 --> 00:00:11.837
Repeated records can inflate counts, totals, averages, and even the size of a machine learning training set.

00:00:11.837 --> 00:00:16.137
The duplicated function can identify repeated values.

00:00:16.137 --> 00:00:19.707
The drop duplicates function can then remove them.

00:00:19.707 --> 00:00:27.752
In the example, we check duplicates using order I D as the business key and keep the first occurrence.

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:32.437
Before deleting anything, confirm the grain of the table.

00:00:32.437 --> 00:00:36.047
Grain means what one row represents.

00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:42.832
If one row represents one order, repeated order I D values may be suspicious.

00:00:42.832 --> 00:00:50.512
If one row represents one product within an order, repeated order I D values may be expected.

00:00:50.512 --> 00:00:54.368
Never deduplicate without understanding the row meaning.
